  "info": {
    "description": "The platform's routing engine for who may say yes, who says it when they do not, and how the answer gets back to the product that asked (ADR-0006). Slice 1: the read-only copy of the reporting line (`hierarchy_nodes`) and the resolved chain endpoint. Requests, steps, delegations and SLA policy land in slice 2.",
    "title": "Approval service",
    "version": "0.1.0"
  },
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "paths": {
    "/api/v1/approvals/chain/{employee_id}": {
      "get": {
        "description": "`core.approval.request.read` \u2014 OWN reaches only the caller's own chain; TEAM reaches a\ndirect report's; LOCATION reaches an employee at one of the caller's locations; ORG reaches\nany.\n\nObject-level check runs before either query: `assert_can_touch` is the same chokepoint every\nother detail endpoint in the platform uses, and a caller outside scope is refused with 403\nbefore the chain walk ever runs (ADR-0003 \u00a73's negative-authz discipline). TEAM and LOCATION\ncompare the *subject's* manager and location against the caller \u2014 never the caller's own \u2014\nso the subject's own hierarchy row is fetched first, ahead of the check, one indexed lookup\nthe walk needs anyway for the response's `location_code`. A subject the copy has not seen\nyet fails TEAM and LOCATION closed (there is nothing to compare against); OWN and ORG are\nunaffected, because neither reads the copy to decide.",
        "operationId": "get_chain_api_v1_approvals_chain__employee_id__get",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "in": "path",
            "name": "employee_id",
            "required": true,
            "schema": {
              "format": "uuid",
              "title": "Employee Id",
              "type": "string"
            }
          }
        ],
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/ApprovalChainOut"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          },
          "422": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/HTTPValidationError"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Validation Error"
          }
        },
        "summary": "The resolved approval chain for one employee",
        "tags": [
          "approval"
        ]
      }
    },
    "/api/v1/approvals/info": {
      "get": {
        "operationId": "info_api_v1_approvals_info_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/ServiceInfo"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          }
        },
        "summary": "What this service is",
        "tags": [
          "approval"
        ]
      }
    },
    "/health": {
      "get": {
        "operationId": "health_health_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "additionalProperties": {
                    "type": "string"
                  },
                  "title": "Response Health Health Get",
                  "type": "object"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          }
        },
        "summary": "Liveness \u2014 is the process up?",
        "tags": [
          "health"
        ]
      }
    },
    "/health/outbox": {
      "get": {
        "description": "How far behind the relay is. **Alert on `oldest_seconds`.**\n\nA stalled relay is the platform's quietest failure. It selects, finds nothing,\nlogs nothing, sleeps, and loops \u2014 `attempts` never increments and `last_error`\nstays null, so the process looks healthy indefinitely while the outbox grows\nwithout bound and every consumer downstream starves.\n\nDeliberately **not** part of readiness. A backlog is not a reason to take the\nservice out of rotation: refusing traffic would stop new events being written\nwithout helping drain the ones already queued, which makes the incident worse.\nThis is a metric to alert on, not a health state.\n\nCounted through the outbox engine, not the application one. The app role is\nNOBYPASSRLS and this request binds no tenant, so through it `pending` would be\nzero whatever the table holds \u2014 the probe that exists to expose the stall would\nbe wired to the same blind spot that causes it.",
        "operationId": "outbox_health_outbox_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "additionalProperties": true,
                  "title": "Response Outbox Health Outbox Get",
                  "type": "object"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          }
        },
        "summary": "Outbox depth and age \u2014 scrape this",
        "tags": [
          "health"
        ]
      }
    },
    "/health/ready": {
      "get": {
        "description": "Route traffic on this; restart on /health.\n\nConflating the two restarts a healthy process that is merely waiting on a\ndependency.\n\nOnly the dependencies a service actually declares are checked. A gateway owns no\ntables, so asking it for a database is asking a question whose answer means nothing\n\u2014 and a readiness probe that reports on something the service does not use is worse\nthan no probe, because it will eventually fail for a reason nobody can act on.",
        "operationId": "ready_health_ready_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "additionalProperties": true,
                  "title": "Response Ready Health Ready Get",
                  "type": "object"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          }
        },
        "summary": "Readiness \u2014 can it serve traffic?",
        "tags": [
          "health"
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
